= Solution
{parent=a}

**A: conjugated <ester>; B: unconjugated <ester>; C: terminal <alkyne> <acetal>.**

A has a strong <ester> C=O band at roughly $1715$–$1730\,\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$, lowered by <conjugation> with C=C. B's unconjugated <ester> <carbonyl> is nearer $1735$–$1750\,\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$; it also has terminal-alkene features, including the approximately $990/910\,\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ pair.

C has **no <carbonyl> band**. Its terminal <alkyne> gives a relatively sharp sp C-H stretch near $3300\,\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ and a usually weaker C≡C feature near $2100$–$2150\,\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$. C-O stretching alone is less diagnostic because <esters> and the <acetal> all contain C-O bonds. The numbers are characteristic ranges, not exact frequencies inferred from an unprinted IR trace.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

**<Carbon-13 NMR> spectra: 1 = C, 2 = A, 3 = B.** Approximate shifts, read from the printed axes, are shown in the annotated structures.

\Image[paper-1-q1-carbon-assignments.png]{title=Derived carbon assignments for the three isomers, excluding the solvent signal near 77 ppm}

* **C:** $\mathrm{HC{\equiv}C-CH(OCH_2CH_3)_2}$ has five <carbon> environments: <acetal> CH near $91$; substituted/terminal <alkyne> <carbons> near $79$ and $74$; OCH$_2$ near $61$; and methyl near $15\,\mathrm{ppm}$. One <alkyne> signal is close to the CDCl$_3$ <solvent> region, which must not be counted as a new molecular environment.
* **A:** $\mathrm{EtOCO-CH=C(CH_3)_2}$ has <ester> <carbonyl> near $167$, substituted <alkene> C near $157$, vinyl CH near $116$, OCH$_2$ near $60$, two nonequivalent <alkene> methyls near $28$ and $21$, and ethoxy methyl near $14\,\mathrm{ppm}$.
* **B:** $\mathrm{CH_2=CH-CH_2-OCO-CH_2-CH_2-CH_3}$ has <carbonyl> near $173$, vinyl CH near $133$, terminal vinyl CH$_2$ near $118$, OCH$_2$ near $65$, acyl-alpha CH$_2$ near $36$, the next CH$_2$ near $19$, and terminal methyl near $14\,\mathrm{ppm}$.

The two methyls in A are chemically distinct because one is cis and the other trans to each substituent at the other <alkene> <carbon>. Their two measured shifts are secure; assigning an individual methyl to a particular side of the drawn double bond requires an additional stereospecific shift argument or experiment, so their spatial interchange is left explicit.

