**A is dimethyl <ether>, $\mathrm{CH_3OCH_3}$**, with NaCl as the corresponding salt byproduct. Backside methoxide attack on methyl chloride gives an <SN2> substitution:
$$
v=k[\mathrm{CH_3Cl}][\mathrm{MeO^-}].
$$
The rate is bimolecular because both reactants participate in the elementary bond-forming/bond-breaking event.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

All comparisons assume the same temperature and comparable reagent thermodynamic activities in <methanol>.

* **Iodomethane:** faster, because iodide is a better <leaving group> and the C-I bond is more readily broken than C-Cl.
* **1-Chloropropane:** slower than methyl chloride for <SN2>, because its ethyl substituent crowds backside approach and raises the substitution barrier. It is still a primary substrate, so <SN2> remains plausible.
* **MeSNa:** generally faster substitution with methanethiolate than methoxide in this <protic solvent>. Sulfur is more polarizable and its larger anion is less strongly hydrogen-bond-solvated, making it a better <nucleophile> here. This must not be inferred simply from basicity: methanethiolate is the weaker base.

