= Solution
{parent=a}

Write both <electrode> reactions first as <reductions>:
$$
\mathrm{CH_3COO^-+5H^++4e^-\longrightarrow C_2H_5OH+H_2O},
$$
$$
\mathrm{CeO_2+4H^++e^-\longrightarrow Ce^{3+}+2H_2O}.
$$
The **conventional cell reaction** runs with <oxidation> on the left and <reduction> on the right. Reverse the first equation and multiply the second by four:
$$
\boxed{\mathrm{C_2H_5OH+4CeO_2+11H^+
\longrightarrow CH_3COO^-+4Ce^{3+}+7H_2O}}.
$$
Four <electrons> pass through the external circuit per reaction as written. This direction consumes the oxide; deposition requires the reverse <electrolysis> direction.

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

$$
E^\circ=E^\circ_{\rm right}-E^\circ_{\rm left}
=1.660-(-0.285)=\boxed{1.945\,\mathrm V}.
$$
With $n=4$,
$$
\Delta_rG^\circ=-nFE^\circ
=-4(96485)(1.945)
=\boxed{-7.51\times10^5\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}}}.
$$
Thus the **conventional forward reaction is spontaneous under standard conditions**, dissolving CeO$_2$ while oxidizing <ethanol>. Oxide deposition is its reverse and needs external electrical work; its intention does not change the sign convention.

= c
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Measure the reversible cell potential at several temperatures while maintaining standard-state thermodynamic activities and the same phases. From $\Delta_rG^\circ=-nFE^\circ$ and $\Delta_rS^\circ=-(\partial\Delta_rG^\circ/\partial T)_p$,
$$
\boxed{\Delta_rS^\circ=nF\left(\frac{\partial E^\circ}{\partial T}\right)_p}.
$$
The sign of the slope gives the sign of the standard reaction <entropy>. Measuring a loaded cell voltage without correcting polarization would not give this reversible thermodynamic derivative.

= d
{parent=4}
{scope}

= i
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Pure solid CeO$_2$ and liquid water have unit <thermodynamic activity> in the stated approximation, so
$$
Q=\frac{a_{\rm Ce^{3+}}^4a_{\rm CH_3COO^-}}
{a_{\rm C_2H_5OH}a_{\rm H^+}^{11}},
\qquad
\boxed{E=E^\circ-\frac{RT}{4F}\ln Q}.
$$
The power 11 and the four-electron denominator follow from the balanced conventional reaction; using the deposition reaction without reversing the potential would give inconsistent signs.

= ii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The fixed nonproton <thermodynamic activity> ratio is $(0.1)^4(0.3)/(3\times10^{-6})=10$. Hence
$$
Q=10\,10^{11\,{\rm pH}},\qquad
E=1.945-\frac{RT\ln10}{4F}(1+11\,{\rm pH}).
$$
Using the supplied constants at $298\,\mathrm K$,
$$
\boxed{E\simeq1.9302-0.1626\,{\rm pH}\quad\mathrm V}.
$$
At <pH> 7, **$E\simeq0.792\,\mathrm V$**. This is the formal fixed-thermodynamic activity calculation requested; actual cerium speciation and thermodynamic activities would need to remain compatible with the chosen <pH> to realize it experimentally.

= e
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

**<Passivation> by the oxide film** is a likely cause. As poorly conducting CeO$_2$ covers <palladium> and thickens, <electron> transport through the deposit becomes difficult and the interfacial resistance rises. Transport of ions/defects through the film can also become limiting.

Thus abundant cerium in bulk solution does not guarantee continued interfacial deposition. The film can block the electronic or ionic path required to grow more oxide.

This is an <oxide-film transport limitation> during <cerium dioxide electrodeposition>.

